WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.777
A PPO is a network of hospitals and physicians that have

00:00:04.777 --> 00:00:09.120
agreed to provide care to plan members at discounted fees.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.897
Members may see any provider, but the plan pays a higher

00:00:13.897 --> 00:00:18.674
share in network and a lower share out of network, and

00:00:18.674 --> 00:00:22.583
in-network providers accept the allowance as payment in full.

00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:26.491
There is no gatekeeper; members see specialists without referrals.

00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:32.571
PPOs are fee-for-service arrangements: the provider is paid per service at the negotiated rate.

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:36.046
Marcus’s employer offers a PPO because it preserves

00:00:36.046 --> 00:00:39.520
choice while the network discounts hold down premiums.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.139
Panel describes who may join the network and whom the providers may treat.

00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:49.462
An open panel PPO or IPA-model HMO contracts with independent

00:00:49.462 --> 00:00:53.784
providers who also treat patients from other plans, and it

00:00:53.784 --> 00:00:57.675
admits any qualified provider willing to accept its terms.

00:00:57.675 --> 00:01:01.565
A closed panel, typical of staff and group model

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:05.455
HMOs, uses providers who serve only that plan’s members.

00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:08.913
For members, open panels mean more choice and

00:01:08.913 --> 00:01:11.939
a better chance their current doctor participates.

00:01:11.939 --> 00:01:16.262
For plans, closed panels mean tighter control over practice patterns.

00:01:16.262 --> 00:01:20.152
Exclusive provider organizations, or EPOs, use a PPO-style network

00:01:20.152 --> 00:01:23.610
but cover nothing out of network except emergencies.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.650
A point-of-service plan is an HMO with an escape hatch.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.690
Inside the network the member uses a primary care physician,

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.729
obtains referrals and pays copayments, exactly as in an HMO.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:40.577
At the point of service, when care is needed, the member may

00:01:40.577 --> 00:01:45.425
instead go outside the network, and the plan pays indemnity-style benefits with

00:01:45.425 --> 00:01:49.869
a deductible and coinsurance, usually leaving the member with higher costs.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:53.908
The POS design answers the biggest objection to HMOs, loss

00:01:53.908 --> 00:01:57.544
of choice, while keeping the gatekeeper for most care.

00:01:57.544 --> 00:02:03.200
Members should understand that out-of-network use often requires them to file their own claims.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.625
Priya asks Theo which design her bakery should offer.

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.147
He frames the trade-off.

00:02:08.147 --> 00:02:10.811
An HMO costs least and restricts most.

00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:13.855
A PPO costs more and lets members see

00:02:13.855 --> 00:02:16.899
specialists anywhere, with incentives to stay in network.

00:02:16.899 --> 00:02:21.465
A POS sits between, with a gatekeeper in network and coverage out.

00:02:21.465 --> 00:02:26.032
An EPO looks like a PPO but pays nothing out of network.

00:02:26.032 --> 00:02:30.217
The right answer depends on the workforce: employees with established specialists

00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:34.023
value PPO freedom; younger employees may prefer the HMO premium.

00:02:34.023 --> 00:02:39.350
Whatever the design, Maryland’s mandated benefits and the federal essential health benefit rules apply.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.607
TRICARE is the Department of Defense health program for active duty

00:02:43.607 --> 00:02:47.864
service members, retirees, National Guard and Reserve members and their families.

00:02:47.864 --> 00:02:49.799
Its options mirror civilian designs.

00:02:49.799 --> 00:02:54.443
TRICARE Prime works like an HMO, with a primary care manager, referrals

00:02:54.443 --> 00:02:58.701
and low out-of-pocket costs, and is mandatory for active duty members.

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:04.119
TRICARE Select works like a PPO, allowing self-referral to network providers with cost sharing.

00:03:04.119 --> 00:03:09.150
TRICARE For Life supplements Medicare for retirees 65 and older, paying after Medicare.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.020
A private health policy generally excludes care TRICARE furnishes, which

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.890
is the government plan exclusion you saw in Lesson 9.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.020
Four clues name the plan.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.854
Does the member need a referral for a specialist?

00:03:22.854 --> 00:03:27.967
Yes points to an HMO or the in-network side of a POS.

00:03:27.967 --> 00:03:30.523
Is there coverage out of network?

00:03:30.523 --> 00:03:36.487
Yes points to a PPO or POS; no points to an HMO or EPO.

00:03:36.487 --> 00:03:40.321
Does the member pay copayments or deductible and coinsurance?

00:03:40.321 --> 00:03:44.155
Copayments lean HMO; coinsurance leans PPO; both appear in

00:03:44.155 --> 00:03:47.564
a POS depending on where care was received.

00:03:47.564 --> 00:03:50.546
Is the payer the Department of Defense?

00:03:50.546 --> 00:03:56.510
Then it is TRICARE, and its option follows the same referral and network clues.
